  "summary": "Polygons pretending to be cels: flat color, black ink, animation confidence.",
  "description": "Cel shading (toon shading) maps 3D geometry onto hand-drawn animation logic: two or three flat color bands per surface, hard terminator edges, and deliberately inked outlines that stay constant width on screen. Jet Set Radio (2000) and Zelda: The Wind Waker (2002) proved the style's legs; Dragon Ball Z, Guilty Gear Xrd and Genshin Impact carried it into anime-gaming mainstream. The craft is in the normals and edge detection — making 3D lie convincingly about being 2D — and its appeal is timelessness: cel-shaded games rarely date as hard as realistic renders.",
  "culturalContext": "Cel shading answered anime fandom's demand for \"the cartoon, playable\": from Jet Set Radio's street culture to Genshin's global anime-game economy, the style defines Japanese game export identity, and its shaders are standard teaching content in game-art education worldwide.",
